@section Values, numbers, and bindings

You already know how to program. This chapter is not about programming; it is
about the specific handful of Rust the rest of the book leans on, taught in
the order the book needs it. Skim what you already recognize and slow down at
the parts that are particular to Rust: ownership in @ref{0.3}, and the
overflow policy this section starts with.

A Rust program names values with @code{let}:

@example rust
let x = 5;
println!("@{x@}");
// => 5
@end example

@code{x} here is a @newterm{binding}, not a variable in the mutable sense:
without the word @code{mut}, it cannot be reassigned. Writing @code{x = 6}
below that line is a compile error, not a runtime one. Rust catches it before
the program ever runs, which is the theme of the next several sections: a
whole class of mistakes moves from ``discovered while running'' to ``rejected
while compiling.''

Two different things look similar and are worth telling apart from the start.
@newterm{Shadowing} introduces a new binding that happens to reuse an old
name; @newterm{mutation} changes the value inside an existing binding that
was declared @code{mut}.

@example rust
let x = 5;
let x = x + 1;
println!("@{x@}");
// => 6

let mut y = 5;
y += 1;
println!("@{y@}");
// => 6
@end example

Both print @code{6}, but they arrived differently. The first @code{let x}
line creates a brand-new binding named @code{x} that shadows the old one; the
old @code{x} still exists underneath it and is simply no longer reachable by
that name. Shadowing can even change type (@code{let x = "5"; let x =
x.len();} is legal). The second block never introduces a second @code{y}:
@code{y} is one binding, marked @code{mut}, and @code{+=} changes the value
it holds in place. Chapter 3's environment model (@ref{0.3}, then chapter 3
in full) is built entirely on this distinction between rebinding a name and
mutating a place.

Two more primitive types round out this section: @code{bool}, exactly
@code{true} or @code{false}, and @code{char}, a single Unicode scalar value
written in single quotes.

@example rust
let is_even: bool = 6 % 2 == 0;
println!("@{is_even@}");
// => true

let initial: char = 'r';
println!("@{initial@}");
// => r
@end example

This chapter also fixes how the book displays what a program computes. Every
runnable listing is an @code{@@example rust} block; a comment starting with
@code{// =>} beneath a line gives the value that line's expression or
@code{println!} produces, exactly as pinned by that listing's test. The
listings above already use it. Section @ref{0.9} covers the convention in
full, including how it maps onto exercise numbers; from here on it needs no
further introduction. The runnable version of these three listings together
is @code{examples/sec_0_1_bindings.rs}.

@subheading Numbers, and the overflow policy

Two number types cover almost everything in this book. @code{i128} is this
edition's one exact integer type, wide enough that the book rarely needs
anything else; @code{f64} is the one inexact real. Chapter 2 adds exact
rationals and complex numbers as an explicit tower on top of @code{i128} and
@code{f64} (@ref{2.5}); you do not need them yet.

Ordinary arithmetic on a fixed-width integer has a sharp edge, and it is
worth knowing exactly where the edge is. In a @newterm{debug} build (the
default for @code{cargo run} and @code{cargo test}), @code{i128::MAX + 1}
panics: Rust checks for overflow and stops the program rather than let it
continue on a wrong answer. In a @newterm{release} build (@code{cargo run
--release}), that same check is compiled out for speed, and the same
expression silently wraps around to a large negative number instead. Neither
behavior is what this book wants: a debug-only panic still crashes a program
that a release build would have corrupted silently, and depending on which
build mode happens to be running is not a design. This book never leaves the
question to build mode. Every integer operation that could overflow uses a
@newterm{checked} method, which trades both possibilities for one explicit
@code{Option}, in every build: @code{Some} on success, @code{None} when the
exact result would not fit.

@example rust
let ok = 1_000_000_000_000i128.checked_mul(1_000_000_000_000);
println!("@{ok:?@}");
// => Some(1000000000000000000000000)

let overflow = i128::MAX.checked_mul(i128::MAX);
println!("@{overflow:?@}");
// => None
@end example

@code{Option} gets its full introduction in @ref{0.4}; for now, read
@code{Some(v)} as ``succeeded with @code{v}'' and @code{None} as ``would have
overflowed.'' This is the number policy the whole book follows: exact
arithmetic is checked, and an overflow is data the program can act on rather
than a silent wrong answer or an unannounced panic. Section @ref{1.2} leans
on exactly this to make the point that unbounded recursion depth, not
@code{i128}'s width, is usually what runs out first.

The runnable version of this listing is
@code{examples/sec_0_1_checked_arithmetic.rs} in this edition's
@code{crates/ch00} crate; @code{cargo run --example
sec_0_1_checked_arithmetic} runs it. Section @ref{0.9} explains this layout.
